#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115040 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115213 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348ff9ef-8ffe-57aa-bafa-9d1a197178fc" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing a Constant on One Side"}] :question/id #uuid "d833825d-4ac4-556d-95a1-bb328d5b5bb4" :question/math-academy-id "e-4149" :question/problem "If $\\log_2(x^2-6)=\\log_2(10),$ then what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}-6) & ={log}_{2}⁡(10) \\\\ {x}^{2}-6 & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=4$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}-6) & ={log}_{2}⁡({4}^{2}-6) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(16-6) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(10).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=4$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-4$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}-6) & ={log}_{2}⁡((-4)^{2}-6) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(16-6) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(10).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-4$ is a valid solution.\n\nSo, we conclude that the solutions are $x = \\pm 4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115042 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115214 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03f79a4-06f3-5324-a1ec-687659956384" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Nonlinear Arguments"}] :question/id #uuid "088cff27-4c06-542c-876b-d3d2ed7b0504" :question/math-academy-id "e-4150" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\ln (2x^2)=\\ln (3x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =3x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3x & =0 \\\\ x(2x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=0$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2(0)^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3(0)) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not valid because $\\ln \\left(0\\right)$ is undefined. Therefore, $x=0$ is not a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3(\\frac{3}{2})) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{2}).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $x =\\dfrac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115043 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115215 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9025fa2d-5f74-5187-a225-234da9b9810b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Linear Arguments on Both Sides"}] :question/id #uuid "d164cff6-3d50-57c8-a118-8681aaf0c1f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-4151" :question/problem "Solve $\\log (x-1)=\\log (2x+1)$ for $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-1) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+1) \\\\ x-1 & =2x+1 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-1) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(-2-1) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2(-2)+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(-3) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\log (-3)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115213 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348ff9ef-8ffe-57aa-bafa-9d1a197178fc" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing a Constant on One Side"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "150e2764-cae5-5097-b0e0-4013ce39bd89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538277} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc865a8-a99f-55ed-b80f-d011253dcedc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538298} {:answer/id #uuid "3b88005d-c042-5ac4-a802-6c3fbb160726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538354} {:answer/id #uuid "71fef0a9-d275-50cc-a7a9-2d08bb857707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538486} {:answer/id #uuid "ed2c6624-a345-5138-8ba0-fd272edd299d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dc865a8-a99f-55ed-b80f-d011253dcedc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538298} :answer-field/id #uuid "f312c34a-9866-5b7f-a9d8-bf68bba44d49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58031be4-0ee8-501f-8ac5-6ad86baa2337" :question/math-academy-id "q-100706" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-2)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}⁡(256).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-2) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}⁡(256) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({256}^{1/4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) \\\\ x-2 & =4 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=6$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-2) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(6-2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({256}^{1/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{log}⁡(256).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=6$ is a valid solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115214 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03f79a4-06f3-5324-a1ec-687659956384" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Nonlinear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79de5745-5bf0-555e-a601-3b494c6bd938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538509} {:answer/id #uuid "8be64e16-04ad-51d8-827e-a16e9b00296b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538560} {:answer/id #uuid "a1508db0-4863-5e06-8a60-94207ae22916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538617} {:answer/id #uuid "add9a8e9-c820-5de7-a37b-45ae258e7857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538642} {:answer/id #uuid "b7237201-cb21-5414-84de-7f2cfcb1a828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1508db0-4863-5e06-8a60-94207ae22916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538617} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0a61e66-3aca-536b-aa8a-9b7a2020142b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ecdce78b-6ad1-5fcc-9999-f701ae74931a" :question/math-academy-id "q-100773" :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}+7)={log}_{2}⁡(2{x}^{2}+8).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}+7) & ={log}_{2}⁡(2{x}^{2}+8) \\\\ {x}^{2}+7 & =2{x}^{2}+8 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${x}^{2}=-1$ has no real solutions. Therefore, our original logarithmic equation has no solutions either."}]}
